Patchsu patch 5 (optional, standalone): switch mod effects off while watching a replay.

Applies on its own to plain osu!lazer (commit 8cb6e6e68d), and also together with any other patch.
0500 (Ritsu mods: One Eye, Decoy Circles, Metronome) and 0501 (Torii mods: Hit Sounder, Pitch Adjust) carry their
own toggles behind PATCHSU_REPLAY_MOD_TOGGLES, which this patch defines in Directory.Build.props, so they switch on
whenever this patch is applied with them (they used to be the separate patches 0007b and 0007c, old numbering).

While a replay plays, the settings panel on the right has a new "Mod effects" group with one checkbox per mod
that can be switched off. The mod icons on screen (top right, and a skin's own mod display) can be clicked to do
the same; a switched-off mod's icon is greyed out. Icons and checkboxes always stay in sync. Unticking a mod only changes what you see and hear: the replay frames, judgements,
score, accuracy, combo, hit timing statistics and spinner spins are exactly the same, and the results screen
still shows the original score. Ticking it again brings the effect back at once.

  Speed:      Half Time, Daycore, Double Time, Nightcore, Wind Up, Wind Down
              The replay plays at normal speed and pitch (Nightcore's drum beat stops too). The playback speed
              slider still works on top. The gameplay rate that judgements and spinners use is untouched.
  Audio:      Muted (track, hit sounds and the metronome go back to normal)
  Visual:     Hidden, Traceable, Flashlight, Blinds, No Scope, Bloom, Grow, Deflate
              Hidden's approach circles and fade-outs come back, and its shorter fade-in is undone too.
              Grow / Deflate objects show at full size, with their approach circles. Bloom's cursor goes back to normal size.

Rulesets: the speed mods, Muted and Flashlight work in every ruleset; Hidden, Traceable, Blinds, No Scope, Bloom, Grow
and Deflate are the osu! ones.

How it works (for later patches):
- IToggleableInReplay (osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods) marks a mod as toggleable. ReplayPlayer is also cached as
  IReplayModEffectToggles (one BindableBool per mod); ModDisplay uses it to show ReplayToggleableModIcon. ReplayPlayer calls
  SetReplayEffectsEnabled(enabled, context) and then refreshes the state transforms of everything on screen.
- Track adjustments: ReplayPlayer applies each toggleable mod to its own AudioAdjustments (still bound into
  AdjustmentsFromMods, so GetTrueGameplayRate never changes) and cancels the switched-off ones on the audible track
  through MasterGameplayClockContainer.ReplayTrackAdjustments (Player.ApplyModToTrack is the new hook).
- Hit object timing (TimePreempt / TimeFadeIn): OsuReplayHitObjectTiming builds a separate playable beatmap without
  the switched-off IAdjustsOsuHitObjectTiming mods and copies only those two values over. Positions, stacking,
  hit windows and object lifetimes are left as they were.
- Other patches put their IToggleableInReplay code inside #if PATCHSU_REPLAY_MOD_TOGGLES (defined here in
  Directory.Build.props), so they still build when this patch is left out.

